Why This Is Asked
Tests practical SRE handling of backlog storms in asynchronous, cross-region pipelines, beyond generic incident response. It emphasizes concrete controls, testability, and safe recovery.

Key Concepts
- Asynchronous pipelines (Kafka, event buses)
- DLQ and consumer lag signals
- Backpressure and throttling policies
- Idempotency and exactly-once semantics
- Canary testing and burst traffic in staging
- Observability: backlog, latency, error ratios, SLOs

Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo dynamic throttling for backlog-aware recovery
class ThrottleController {
  constructor(maxRate) { this.rate = maxRate; this.maxRate = maxRate; this.minRate = 0.1; }
  adjust(state) {
    if (state.backlog >= state.targetBacklog) this.rate = Math.max(this.minRate, this.rate * 0.8);
    else if (state.lag <= state.targetLag) this.rate = Math.min(this.maxRate, this.rate * 1.25);
    applyRate(state.topic, this.rate);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical debugging in a real-world, multi-region environment with tenant isolation. Emphasizes telemetry-driven root-cause analysis, dynamic traffic shaping, and controlled validation before rollback.

## Key Concepts

- Telemetry-driven debugging across regions
- Per-tenant isolation and rate limiting
- Cache partitioning and traffic shifting
- Progressive degradation with safe fallbacks
- Canary and synthetic test-based validation
- Rollback criteria tied to SLOs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: rate limiter sketch for per-tenant edge gateway
class RateLimiter {
  constructor(limitPerTenant) { this.limit = limitPerTenant; this.map = new Map(); }
  allow(tenantId) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const rec = this.map.get(tenantId) || { t: now, c: 0 };
    if (now - rec.t > 1000) { rec.t = now; rec.c = 0; }
    if (rec.c < this.limit) { rec.c++; this.map.set(tenantId, rec); return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

